  "requestTimestamp": "2026-07-19T01:22:59.474Z",
  "answer": "### Short Answer\nA minimum-wage earner with a single employer is not required to file an annual income tax return if their income tax has been correctly withheld by the employer. This is due to the substituted filing provision under the NIRC.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nUnder Section 51-A of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC), individual taxpayers receiving purely compensation income from only one employer, where the income tax has been withheld correctly, are not required to file an annual income tax return. The employer's certificate of withholding serves as the substituted filing.\n\n### Interpretation\nSection 51-A explicitly states that employees whose income tax has been accurately withheld by their employer are exempt from the obligation to file an annual income tax return. This provision simplifies compliance for minimum-wage earners and ensures that their tax obligations are met through withholding.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nFor minimum-wage earners, this means they can avoid the complexities of filing an income tax return, provided their employer has withheld the correct amount of tax. The employer's certificate of withholding will suffice for tax purposes, reducing administrative burdens for both the employee and the Bureau of Internal Revenue (BIR).",
